package main

type lesson struct {
	title, source, body, example, question string
	choices                                []string
	correct                                int
	explanation                            string
}

var lessons = []lesson{
	{"Meet the tiles", "Beginner introduction; Book I, §3.2", "A set has 136 tiles: four copies of 34 types. Characters, dots and bamboo each run from 1 to 9. The seven honors are four winds and three dragons. Honors have no numerical order.\n\nNumbers 2–8 are simples. The 1s and 9s are terminals. Start by recognizing the suits; you do not need to read Japanese.", "123m123p123s1234567z", "Which tiles are terminals?", []string{"Only 1 and 9 in each suit", "All winds and dragons", "Numbers 2 through 8"}, 0, "Terminals are the 1s and 9s. Honors are a separate category."},
	{"Build a winning hand", "Book I, §3.2", "A standard hand has four groups and one pair: 14 tiles. A group is a run of three consecutive tiles in one suit, or a set of three identical tiles. A pair is two identical tiles.\n\nHonors can form sets and pairs, never runs. Seven distinct pairs and thirteen orphans are special winning shapes. Every win also needs a yaku.", "123m456p789s777z55p", "Which group is a valid run?", []string{"East, South, West", "7, 8, 9 of bamboo", "8, 9, 1 of dots"}, 1, "A run stays in one suit and never wraps from 9 back to 1."},
	{"Take your turn", "Beginner introduction", "Four players take turns counterclockwise. You normally hold 13 tiles. Draw one tile, then discard one face up. A completed hand has 14 tiles.\n\nTsumo means winning on your own draw. Ron means winning on another player's discard. The dealer is East. The practice table here is a solo draw-and-discard exercise, so you can learn without a timer.", "123m456p23467s55p", "How many tiles do you hold after discarding?", []string{"12", "13", "14"}, 1, "Draw to 14, then discard back to 13. A winning hand keeps the fourteenth tile."},
	{"Recognize your waits", "Book I, §3.4", "Tenpai means one tile from a complete hand. With 4–5 of a suit, either 3 or 6 finishes a run: a two-sided wait. With 4–6, only 5 fills the gap. With 1–2, only 3 finishes the edge.\n\nTwo-sided waits often have up to eight winning tiles before accounting for visible copies. A pair wait has only three other copies. Count available tiles as well as tile types.", "45s", "Which tiles complete 4–5 bamboo?", []string{"3 or 6 bamboo", "Only 6 bamboo", "4 or 5 bamboo"}, 0, "Both 3–4–5 and 4–5–6 are valid runs. This is a two-sided wait (ryanmen)."},
	{"Think in five blocks", "Book I, Chapter 4", "Look for five useful blocks: four future groups and a pair. Keep complete groups, strong two-sided shapes, and a possible head. Compare the weakest blocks before removing a tile.\n\nAn isolated honor does not connect to number tiles. In this example East is extra: removing it keeps three complete runs, a pair and a two-sided shape. This is a teaching example, not a universal rule to discard honors.", "123m456p23467s55p1z", "Which tile is outside the five useful blocks?", []string{"5 dots", "East", "6 bamboo"}, 1, "East is isolated. Keep 123 characters, 456 dots, 234 bamboo, 67 bamboo and the pair of 5 dots."},
	{"Find a yaku", "Book I, Chapter 5", "A complete shape alone is not enough: you need at least one yaku. Tanyao uses only simples. Yakuhai is a set of dragons, your seat wind, or the round wind. Menzen tsumo is a self-drawn win with a closed hand.\n\nPinfu requires a closed hand, four runs, a non-value pair and a two-sided winning wait. Dora adds han, but never supplies the required yaku.", "234m456p234678s55p", "Can dora alone make a hand legal to win?", []string{"Yes, with two dora", "Yes, if the shape is complete", "No, a yaku is still required"}, 2, "Dora is a bonus. You still need a yaku such as riichi, tanyao or yakuhai."},
	{"Declare riichi", "Book I, Chapter 7", "With a closed hand in tenpai, you can declare riichi and pay a 1,000-point deposit. It gives one han. You need at least 1,000 points and enough tiles left in the live wall.\n\nAfter declaring, your hand is locked: normally discard each new draw unless you win. Riichi announces your threat and limits your defense. Learn good waits before deciding when to declare.", "123m456p23467s55p", "What must be true before declaring riichi?", []string{"Your hand is closed and in tenpai", "You have called a run", "You have at least one dora"}, 0, "Riichi needs a closed, ready hand. Open calls normally remove that option."},
	{"Understand the payment", "Book I, Chapter 6", "First count han from yaku and bonuses. Then find fu from the hand's structure. Finally use han, fu, win type and dealer status to get the payment. Payments round up to 100 points.\n\nPinfu tsumo has 20 fu; pinfu ron has 30 fu. Seven pairs always has 25 fu. A 5-han mangan pays 8,000 on non-dealer ron or 12,000 on dealer ron. The scoring lab shows Agari's exact breakdown.", "123m456p234678s55p", "What is non-dealer mangan worth on ron?", []string{"4,000 points", "8,000 points", "12,000 points"}, 1, "The discarder pays 8,000. On dealer ron, mangan is 12,000. Honba and deposits are separate."},
	{"Defend and call carefully", "Book I, Chapters 8–9", "When an opponent declares riichi, compare your hand's speed and value with the risk of pushing. Genbutsu means a tile that opponent has discarded; it is safe from their ron because of furiten. It may still be dangerous to the other players.\n\nChi takes a run from the player on your left. Pon takes a set from any player. These calls open your hand. Keep a route to a yaku and a reason to call: speed, value, or both.", "222p123m777z", "An opponent discarded 2 dots before declaring riichi. Is 2 dots safe?", []string{"Safe from that opponent's ron", "Safe from every opponent", "Guaranteed to be in the wall"}, 0, "It is genbutsu against that player. Safety is specific to an opponent, not the whole table."},
}

var glossary = [][2]string{
	{"Yaku", "A qualifying pattern or condition required to win. Dora is a bonus, not a yaku."},
	{"Tenpai", "A hand one tile from a complete winning shape. Shanten 0."},
	{"Riichi", "A 1,000-point declaration with a closed tenpai hand; normally locks your discards."},
	{"Furiten", "You cannot ron if any tile completing your current wait appears in your own discards. Passing ron also causes temporary furiten; after riichi it lasts for the hand. Tsumo remains possible."},
	{"Chi / pon / kan", "Call a run / set / quad. Chi is from the left player only. Kan needs replacement-draw and dora rules; it is not available in the solo table."},
	{"Dora", "A bonus tile, one han per copy. A suited indicator points to the next rank, wrapping 9 to 1. Winds cycle East–South–West–North; dragons cycle White–Green–Red."},
	{"Pinfu", "Closed hand, four runs, a non-value pair, and a two-sided wait. 1 han."},
	{"Tanyao", "All simples: only numbers 2–8. 1 han; open tanyao is allowed in the rules used here."},
	{"Yakuhai", "A set of a dragon, your seat wind or the round wind. 1 han for each qualifying role."},
	{"Seven pairs", "Seven distinct pairs, a special closed shape. 2 han and exactly 25 fu."},
	{"Thirteen orphans", "One of every terminal and honor, plus a duplicate of one of them. A special yakuman shape."},
	{"Genbutsu", "A tile safe from a particular opponent's ron because of their discards and furiten."},
}
